  "query_preview": "..\nAn upper ridge that has kept the area warmer than normal will be\nmoving to our east as a trough pattern will move into the western\nstates for the beginning of colder conditions.\n\nThe upper trough will bring with it an area of precipitation that\nmoves into the western CWA late Tuesday night with the bulk of it\nfalling Wednesday. Most of it will be in the form of rain but\nhigher elevation areas in Phillips and northern Valley counties\nmay mix with or changeover to snow. The models came in drier this\nrun but they may waffle a bit before the precipitation arrives. \n\nAmounts for SW Phillips county have dropped but 2 inches still \nseem possible, so will keep current Winter Weather Advisory going \n(in effect 11 pm Tuesday till 5 Wednesday).\n\nLocations along the Canadian Border west of Scobey could see up to\nan inch of snow. Snow amounts will depend on the timing of the \nrain to mixed precipitation and snow transitions. EC, GFS, and \nCMC ensembles continue to have varying higher snowfall amounts \nespecially NW of line from Zortman, Malta to Opheim so the snow \nforecast will need to watched in future model runs. As the main \nupper trough settles into the intermountain west Wednesday and \nThursday cooling aloft will drive temperatures closer to normal.\n\nLarge scale upper troughing continues across the western US the\nremainder of the the week. The next system will bring another shot\nof colder air from the north. Some light snow is possible but the\nmain impact will be the below normal temps.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Raised QPF and snow amounts from NBM Tuesday night and Wednesday\n  toward WPC Guidance to keep messaging consistent.\n\n- The rest of the forecasted rested with what the NBM gave.\n\n- Confidence in the pattern change on Tuesday night and Wednesday is\n  high.\n\n- Confidence in precipitation Tuesday night and Wednesday is high.\n  TFJ",
